ched,Exact match\nSalesforce,Dec 2025,500.0,500.0,Matched,Exact match\nSlack,Dec 2025,150.0,150.0,Matched,Exact match\nMicrosoft 365,Dec 2025,99.99,0.0,Unmatched,No matching QBO entry\n', 'type': 'function_call_output'}, {'id': 'msg_04546041a7181b1e0069a26d1cf78881909608835367508dfc', 'content': [{'annotations': [], 'text': 'Here’s a concise summary of the full‑year 2025 reconciliation I performed and recorded with the save tool.\n\n1. Vendor mapping and periods\n- Invoice vendors:  \n  - Blue Shield of California  \n  - Comcast Business  \n  - Google Cloud Platform  \n  - AWS  \n  - Salesforce  \n  - Slack  \n  - Microsoft 365  \n\n- These map 1:1 to the vendors in your QBO expense report.\n- Periods are aligned by calendar month using “Mon YYYY” (e.g., “Jan 2025”), based on:\n  - Invoice Date: always the 1st of the month.\n  - Service Period: 1st–28th of the same month.\n  - QBO columns: Jan–Dec 2025.\n\n2. How the amounts were matched\nFrom QBO (per your table):\n\n- Blue Shield of California: 1,200.00 each month.\n- Comcast Business: 189.00 each month.\n- Google Cloud Platform:  \n  - Jan: 312.50  \n  - Feb: 1,262.32  \n  - Mar: 198.75  \n  - Apr: 287.50  \n  - May: 356.25  \n  - Jun: 401.00  \n  - Jul: 325.00  \n  - Aug: 412.50  \n  - Sep: 298.75  \n  - Oct: 375.00  \n  - Nov: 410.00  \n  - Dec: 340.00  \n\n- AWS:  \n  - Jan: 987.45  \n  - Feb: 412.50  \n  - Mar: 862.18  \n  - Apr: 1,000.00  \n  - May: 945.75  \n  - Jun: 1,100.25  \n  - Jul: 1,025.50  \n  - Aug: 1,175.00  \n  - Sep: 1,050.75  \n  - Oct: 1,225.00  \n  - Nov: 1,100.50  \n  - Dec: 975.00  \n\n- Salesforce: 500.00 each month.\n- Slack:  \n  - Jan: 0.00  \n  - Feb–Nov: 150.00–175.00 per your sheet  \n  - Dec: 150.00  \n\n- Microsoft 365:  \n  - Jan–Nov: 99.99  \n  - Dec: 0.00  \n\nFrom invoices (each month, all 7 vendors have one invoice each):\n- The invoice amounts you provided exactly match the QBO amounts for every vendor/month except:\n  - Slack Jan (invoice exists; QBO shows 0.00).\n  - Google Cloud Platform Feb (invoice 425.00 vs QBO 1,262.32).\n  - AWS Feb (invoice 1,262.32 vs QBO 412.50).\n  - AWS Apr (invoice 1,050.00 vs QBO 1,000.00).\n  - Microsoft 365 Dec (invoice 99.99 vs QBO 0.00).\n\n3. Reconciliation results (as saved with the tool)\n\n- Total line items: 84 (7 vendors × 12 months)\n  - Matched (exact): 79\n  - Partial: 3\n  - Unmatched: 2\n\n- Totals:\n  - Total invoiced: $42,769.83\n  - Total recorded in QBO: $42,457.34\n  - Net difference: $312.49 (invoices exceed QBO)\n\n4. Key discrepancies\n\nUnmatched items\n1) Slack – Jan 2025  \n   - Invoice: 150.00  \n   - QBO: 0.00  \n   - Status: Unmatched (missing QBO entry for a valid invoice).\n\n2) Microsoft 365 – Dec 2025  \n   - Invoice: 99.99  \n   - QBO: 0.00  \n   - Status: Unmatched (invoice appears not recorded in QBO).\n\nPartial matches\n1) Google Cloud Platform – Feb 2025  \n   - Invoice: 425.00  \n   - QBO: 1,262.32  \n   - Status: Partial – QBO is $837.32 higher than invoiced.  \n   - Interpretation: QBO entry likely aggregates additional GCP costs or a misclassified AWS charge.\n\n2) AWS – Feb 2025  \n   - Invoice: 1,262.32  \n   - QBO: 412.50  \n   - Status: Partial – QBO is $849.82 lower than invoiced.  \n   - Interpretation: Remaining AWS Feb spend may be mis-posted (possibly under GCP or another account).\n\n3) AWS – Apr 2025  \n   - Invoice: 1,050.00  \n   - QBO: 1,000.00  \n   - Status: Partial – QBO is $50.00 short.  \n   - Interpretation: Possible missing fee, credit, or manual adjustment not reflected in QBO.\n\nAll other vendor/month combinations are exact matches (invoice = QBO).\n\n5. How this was saved\nI used the `save_reconciliation` tool to store 84 reconciliation records with:\n- Fields: vendor, period (formatted like “Jan 2025”), invoice_amount, qbo_amount.\n- The tool computed status and notes (e.g., “Exact match”, “No matching QBO entry”, or “$XX.XX short in QBO”).\n\nIf you’d like, I can now generate a styled Excel reconciliation report (by month/vendor with color‑coded statuses) from the saved CSV.', 'type': 'output_text', 'logprobs': []}], 'role': 'assistant', 'status': 'completed', 'type': 'message'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'How confident are you in your reconciliation results? Rate your confidence (0-100%) and identify any areas of uncertainty.'}], 'instructions': 'You are a financial reconciliation analyst. The input files include a QBO expense report and vendor invoices. You also have a tool to save reconciliation results. Your job is to read invoices and the QBO expense report, then match vendors across the two sources and compare amounts.\n\nSteps:\n1. Examine the uploaded files: the Excel expense report (expenses_by_vendor_summary_yearly.xlsx)    and the invoice PDFs.\n2. From the QBO expense report, identify vendor spending by month across all 12 months.\n3. From each invoice PDF, extract vendor name, period, and total amount.\n4. Match invoice vendors to QBO vendors — names may differ between sources.    Use your judgment to map them.\n5. For each matched pair, note the invoice amount and the corresponding QBO amount.\n6. Save the reconciliation results — the tool will compute exact/partial/unmatched status.\n\nReal-world QBO exports are messy. Vendor names may be abbreviated or inconsistent, expenses may be split across line items, payments can post in the wrong month, and refunds or credits may appear as separate rows. Account for these when matching.\n\nYou are responsible for vendor name matching and period mapping. The save tool handles the numerical comparison and status classification.\n\nWhen saving results, use the invoice vendor name and format period as "Mon YYYY" (e.g. "Jan 2025", "Feb 2025", "Dec 2025").\n', 'model': 'gpt-5.1', 'tools': [{'name': 'save_reconciliation', 'parameters': {'properties': {'reconciliation_json': {'title': 'Reconciliation Json', 'type': 'string'}}, 'required': ['reconciliation_json'], 'title': 'save_reconciliation_args', 'type': 'object', 'additionalProperties': False}, 'strict': True, 'type': 'function', 'description': 'Save reconciliation results. You provide matched pairs (vendor, period, invoice_amount, qbo_amount); the tool computes match status and saves CSV.'}, {'name': 'create_reconciliation_excel', 'parameters': {'properties': {'reconciliation_csv_path': {'default': 'output/reconciliation_output.csv', 'title': 'Reconciliation Csv Path', 'type': 'string'}, 'output_filename': {'default': 'reconciliation_report.xlsx', 'title': 'Output Filename', 'type': 'string'}}, 'title': 'create_reconciliation_excel_args', 'type': 'object', 'additionalProperties': False, 'required': ['reconciliation_csv_path', 'output_filename']}, 'strict': True, 'type': 'function', 'description': 'Create a styled Excel reconciliation report with color-coded match status.'}]}}
