ded our full-year 2025 (Jan-Dec) vendor invoices and our QBO expense report. This is a large, comprehensive dataset spanning 12 months with 84 invoices from 7 vendors.\n\nReconcile all invoices against the QBO report. Some vendors may have partial matches or missing QBO entries — identify and document these discrepancies.\n\nReview the uploaded files, extract the relevant data from the expense report and invoices, and use the save tool to record your reconciliation results.\n\n\n[Run ID: 019ca27b-7762-7379-bfe9-1807060dac1d]'}]}], 'instructions': 'You are a financial reconciliation analyst. The input files include a QBO expense report and vendor invoices. You also have a tool to save reconciliation results. Your job is to read invoices and the QBO expense report, then match vendors across the two sources and compare amounts.\n\nSteps:\n1. Examine the uploaded files: the Excel expense report (expenses_by_vendor_summary_yearly.xlsx)    and the invoice PDFs.\n2. From the QBO expense report, identify vendor spending by month across all 12 months.\n3. From each invoice PDF, extract vendor name, period, and total amount.\n4. Match invoice vendors to QBO vendors — names may differ between sources.    Use your judgment to map them.\n5. For each matched pair, note the invoice amount and the corresponding QBO amount.\n6. Save the reconciliation results — the tool will compute exact/partial/unmatched status.\n\nReal-world QBO exports are messy. Vendor names may be abbreviated or inconsistent, expenses may be split across line items, payments can post in the wrong month, and refunds or credits may appear as separate rows. Account for these when matching.\n\nYou are responsible for vendor name matching and period mapping. The save tool handles the numerical comparison and status classification.\n\nWhen saving results, use the invoice vendor name and format period as "Mon YYYY" (e.g. "Jan 2025", "Feb 2025", "Dec 2025").\n', 'model': 'gpt-5.1', 'tools': [{'name': 'save_reconciliation', 'parameters': {'properties': {'reconciliation_json': {'title': 'Reconciliation Json', 'type': 'string'}}, 'required': ['reconciliation_json'], 'title': 'save_reconciliation_args', 'type': 'object', 'additionalProperties': False}, 'strict': True, 'type': 'function', 'description': 'Save reconciliation results. You provide matched pairs (vendor, period, invoice_amount, qbo_amount); the tool computes match status and saves CSV.'}, {'name': 'create_reconciliation_excel', 'parameters': {'properties': {'reconciliation_csv_path': {'default': 'output/reconciliation_output.csv', 'title': 'Reconciliation Csv Path', 'type': 'string'}, 'output_filename': {'default': 'reconciliation_report.xlsx', 'title': 'Output Filename', 'type': 'string'}}, 'title': 'create_reconciliation_excel_args', 'type': 'object', 'additionalProperties': False, 'required': ['reconciliation_csv_path', 'output_filename']}, 'strict': True, 'type': 'function', 'description': 'Create a styled Excel reconciliation report with color-coded match status.'}]}}
